export const introduction = [
  "Squib testing is the measurement of bridgewire resistance and continuity in a squib, initiator or its firing circuit, using a test current held far below the level that would initiate the device, to confirm that the device and its circuit are intact without setting them off. It is non-destructive: the device is left exactly as it was found.",
  "The measurement is taken by passing current through the exact element that fires the device, and there is no way to measure around it. That is why an ordinary multimeter or insulation tester must never be connected to a squib, and why the instrument’s current ceiling under fault matters more than its accuracy.",
  "This page covers aircraft egress and escape systems, stores release, fire suppression cartridges, and the ground test of those circuits. The same measurement discipline exists in automotive airbag inflator work and in commercial blasting; neither is covered here, and the standards and approval environment differ in both.",
  "This page explains how the test works and how to select equipment for it, not how to perform one. The device’s own specification and the operator’s approved maintenance manual govern any actual test, including which values are acceptable.",
];

